# Episode 1 script: (shot, chapter-title-or-None, narration)
# Narration uses spelled-out years so Kokoro reads them correctly.
SCRIPT = [
 ("attack", None, "Just before one in the morning, on the twenty-second of October, nineteen-oh-four, a fleet of Russian battleships turned its searchlights onto a dark patch of the North Sea. The gunners saw enemy torpedo boats. They opened fire."),
 ("night_fleet", None, "There was just one problem. There were no torpedo boats. There weren't even any Japanese ships. They were shooting at fishermen from Hull."),
 ("title", None, "This is the story of the Dogger Bank incident. The night the Russian Navy nearly started a war with Britain by attacking a fishing fleet. And how that was only the beginning of one of the most disastrous voyages in naval history."),

 ("map_a", "A War on the Other Side of the World", "In nineteen-oh-four, Russia and Japan were at war over Manchuria and Korea. And for Russia, it was going badly. Its Pacific fleet was trapped at Port Arthur, and the Japanese navy controlled the sea."),
 ("port_arthur", None, "The war had started in February, nineteen-oh-four, with a surprise. At night, Japanese destroyers slipped up to Port Arthur and fired torpedoes at Russian warships lying at anchor, with their lights on. That memory, a torpedo attack out of the dark with no warning, would haunt the Russian Navy for the rest of the war."),
 ("port_arthur", None, "Over the following months, Japanese ships blockaded the port, while the Japanese army closed in on land. Russia's Pacific battleships were stuck, and help by rail across Siberia was painfully slow. If Russia wanted to win back the sea, it needed a new fleet. And the only one it had was on the other side of the world."),
 ("fleet_day", None, "So the Tsar approved a bold plan. Ships from the Baltic Fleet would become the Second Pacific Squadron, and sail to the Far East to turn the war around. The only issue was distance. The route was roughly eighteen thousand nautical miles. Around Europe, around Africa, and across the Indian Ocean."),
 ("fleet_day", None, "The man in charge was Admiral Zinovy Rozhestvensky. Strict, short-tempered, and with few illusions about his fleet. Some ships were brand new and untested. Many crews were barely trained. During exercises before departure, ships even managed to collide with each other."),
 ("map_b", None, "On the fifteenth of October, nineteen-oh-four, the squadron left Libau, on the Baltic coast. Around forty warships and support vessels, heading for a war on the other side of the planet."),

 ("rumours", "The Fear", "And from the moment they left, they were afraid. Russian intelligence claimed Japanese officers were lurking around the Baltic with torpedoes. That Britain, Japan's ally, had built torpedo boats for Japan. That there were mines in the straits, and submarines in the water."),
 ("rumours", None, "None of it was true. But to exhausted crews staring into the dark, every light on the horizon looked like an attack. Near the Danish coast, the fleet even fired on fishermen who were carrying dispatches from the Russian consulate. Luckily, their aim was terrible."),
 ("rumours", None, "The day before Dogger Bank, ships reported balloons in the sky, and four enemy cruisers. Then came a report of torpedo boats leaving Norway. Rozhestvensky ordered that no vessel was to be allowed among the fleet."),

 ("kamchatka", "The Kamchatka", "On the evening of the twenty-first of October, the fleet was crossing the North Sea. At the very back of the line was the Kamchatka, a slow repair ship. That evening, the Kamchatka sent an urgent message. It was under attack by torpedo boats."),
 ("kamchatka", None, "In reality, the Kamchatka had come across a Swedish merchant ship, a German trawler, and a French schooner. It fired on them anyway. Its crew later claimed to have fought off three Japanese warships, firing more than three hundred shells. Now the rest of the fleet believed the enemy was out there. Somewhere ahead, in the dark."),

 ("night_fleet", "Dogger Bank", "The Dogger Bank is a vast shallow sandbank in the middle of the North Sea. It's one of the richest fishing grounds in Europe. And that night, the Gamecock fleet from Hull was there. Dozens of small steam trawlers, working through the night with their nets down."),
 ("night_fleet", None, "At five to one in the morning, in fog, Rozhestvensky spotted small boats with no lights near the squadron. He ordered a change of course, and the searchlights came on."),
 ("attack", None, "In the glare, the trawlers lit up. Some of the fishermen came on deck to see what was happening. Then the admiral believed he saw a torpedo boat. The order came. Open fire."),
 ("attack", None, "The trawlers couldn't run. Their nets were down, dragging along the seabed. For somewhere between ten and twenty minutes, depending on whose account you trust, the battleships poured fire into the fishing fleet."),
 ("crane", None, "The trawler Crane was hit, and began to sink. Her skipper, George Henry Smith, and third hand, William Leggett, were killed. Other boats were damaged, and six fishermen were wounded. One of them, Walter Whelpton, skipper of the Mino, died months later."),
 ("aurora", None, "But the chaos didn't stop there. In the darkness, Russian battleships mistook two of their own cruisers, the Aurora and the Dmitri Donskoi, for Japanese warships, and opened fire on them too. A priest and at least one sailor were killed. By their own fleet."),
 ("aurora", None, "The shooting was so wild that one battleship, the Oryol, reportedly fired more than five hundred shells without hitting anything at all. Finally, a signal light ordered the guns to cease fire. And then the Russian fleet simply sailed on. It didn't stop to help."),

 ("hull", "The Russian Outrage", "When the surviving trawlers limped back into Hull with their dead and wounded, Britain exploded. Some newspapers called the Russians pirates. The incident got a new name. The Russian Outrage."),
 ("paper1", None, "The Times was furious. Crowds were angry. And the question everyone was asking was simple. How could an entire battle fleet mistake fishing boats for warships, in the middle of the North Sea?"),
 ("britain", None, "The Royal Navy ordered twenty-eight battleships of the Home Fleet to raise steam. British cruisers shadowed the Russian squadron down through the Bay of Biscay and along the coast of Portugal. For a few days, the two empires were on the edge of war."),
 ("vigo", None, "Under pressure, Russia agreed to an investigation. At Vigo, in Spain, Rozhestvensky left behind the officers held responsible, and on the first of November, the fleet sailed on. By the eleventh of November, the British government told the navy that the crisis was over."),
 ("paper2", None, "The case went to an international commission of inquiry in Paris, set up under the Hague Convention. It sat from January to February, nineteen-oh-five. In the end, its report did not discredit the admiral or his men. But Russia paid sixty-six thousand pounds to the fishermen. That's millions in today's money."),

 ("map_c", "The Long Road to Tsushima", "As for the fleet, its voyage was only getting started. Near Tangier, a Russian ship's anchor cut the underwater telegraph cable, cutting off communications with Europe for four days. Then the fleet split. The newest battleships went around Africa and the Cape of Good Hope. Older ships went through the Suez Canal. They met up again at Madagascar."),
 ("madagascar", "Stranded at Madagascar", "Rozhestvensky's ships reached Nosy Be, off Madagascar, at the end of December. Then, just days later, came terrible news. On the second of January, nineteen-oh-five, Port Arthur surrendered. The fleet the squadron was sailing to rescue no longer existed. Its whole mission had disappeared."),
 ("madagascar", None, "Instead of turning back, the fleet was ordered to wait, while Saint Petersburg put together reinforcements, a Third Pacific Squadron, made up largely of old and slow ships. So the sailors sat in the tropical heat for weeks, then months. Coal ran low, ships broke down, and discipline cracked. At least one mutiny had to be put down by force."),
 ("map_d", None, "On the sixteenth of March, the fleet finally left Madagascar and crossed the Indian Ocean. Off the coast of French Indochina, it was joined by the old ships of Admiral Nebogatov. Seven months after leaving the Baltic, worn out and short of everything, the Russians made for Vladivostok, through the Tsushima Strait."),
 ("battle", "Tsushima", "Waiting for them was Admiral Togo Heihachiro, and a Japanese fleet that had spent months training, repairing, and resting. Just three days before the battle, Rozhestvensky's second-in-command, Admiral Fölkersahm, died at sea. Rozhestvensky kept it quiet. He didn't even tell Admiral Nebogatov, who was now, without knowing it, next in line to command."),
 ("tsushima", None, "On the twenty-seventh of May, nineteen-oh-five, in the Tsushima Strait between Korea and Japan, they met Admiral Togo and the Japanese fleet. It was a catastrophe. Rozhestvensky was wounded early in the battle. Over two days, almost the whole squadron was sunk, captured, or driven into neutral ports. Russia lost more than five thousand men. The wounded admiral was taken prisoner."),
 ("tsushima", None, "The fleet that had crossed half the world, and opened fire on fishermen along the way, never arrived as a fighting force. Japan, by contrast, lost just three torpedo boats, and a hundred and seventeen men."),
 ("battle", None, "The next morning, Admiral Nebogatov, surrounded with his old ships, surrendered. Only a handful of Russian ships escaped. One cruiser and two destroyers reached Vladivostok. Three cruisers made it to Manila, where the Americans interned them until the end of the war."),

 ("treaty", "Aftermath", "The defeat was so complete that Russia agreed to peace talks. With President Theodore Roosevelt as mediator, Russia and Japan signed the Treaty of Portsmouth on the fifth of September, nineteen-oh-five. Roosevelt won the Nobel Peace Prize for it. Japan became the first Asian power in the modern era to defeat a European great power in a major war."),
 ("court", None, "Back in Russia, someone had to be blamed. Rozhestvensky and his officers faced courts martial. The admiral, who'd been unconscious from his wounds when his destroyer surrendered, was acquitted. Admiral Nebogatov was not so lucky. He was sentenced to death, though the sentence was commuted, and he was later released."),
 ("paper2", None, "And the Dogger Bank inquiry? It quietly became important in its own right. Two great powers, furious and close to war, had handed the dispute to an international commission instead of fighting over it. It's often remembered as an early example of countries settling a crisis through an inquiry rather than a war."),

 ("aurora_epi", "What Remains", "And the Aurora? The cruiser shelled by its own side at Dogger Bank escaped from Tsushima to Manila. Years later, in nineteen seventeen, it fired the famous blank shot linked to the start of the October Revolution. Today, it's a museum ship in Saint Petersburg."),
 ("memorial", None, "And in Hull, in nineteen-oh-six, the fishermen got a memorial. About eighteen feet high, it honours the three men who died on the night a battle fleet mistook a fishing fleet for an enemy armada."),
 ("outro", None, "That was the Dogger Bank incident. If you enjoyed it, subscribe to Blunderbuss History for the full stories of history's biggest blunders. See you in the next one."),
]
